<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Islamic schools and branches</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Islamic schools and branches are the sects, schools of jurisprudence and schools of theology into which Muslims are divided. The original schism between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharijites">Kharijites</a>, Sunnīs and Shīʿas arose over the succession to the guidance of the Muslim community after the death of Muhammad: Shīʿas hold ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib to be his true successor, Sunnīs Abu Bakr. Sunnīs, around 87-90% of all Muslims, follow four main schools of jurisprudence, the Mālikī, Ḥanafī, Shāfiʿī and Ḥanbalī; Shīʿa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> has three major sects, the Twelvers, Ismāʿīlīs and Zaydīs. Of the Kharijite branches only the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibadism">Ibadis</a> survive.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The earliest divisions among Muslims concerned succession to the guidance of the community after Muhammad's death. Shīʿas hold ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, Muhammad's cousin and son-in-law, to be the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a> and rightful successor, and reject the legitimacy of the first three Rāshidūn caliphs. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharijites">Kharijites</a> arose during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-fitna">First Fitna</a>, the struggle that followed the assassination of the third caliph Uthman in 656. They first supported ʿAlī, later fought against him and killed him in the mosque of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>. Apart from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibadism">Ibadis</a>, whose roots go back to them, they are extinct. The Murji'ah held that only God can judge who is a true Muslim.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Sunnīs follow four main schools of jurisprudence, the Ḥanafī, Mālikī, Shāfiʿī and Ḥanbalī, named after scholars of the 8th century CE, which differ in how they derive rulings from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>, ḥadīth and sunnah. In theology the Atharī, Ashʿarī and Māturīdī schools stand within Sunnism. Shīʿa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> is a majority in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, and its main jurisprudence is the Jaʿfari school, named after the sixth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a> and divided into the Usuli school, which favours <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ijtihad">ijtihad</a>, and the Akhbari school. The Ismāʿīlīs and Zaydīs are smaller Shīʿa groups.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Mu'tazila, who arose in 8th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a>, sought to combine Greek rationalism with Islamic doctrine. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufism</a>, the mystical dimension of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, is represented by orders known as ṭarīqah. Later movements include the Ahmadiyya, founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> in 1889 by Mirza Ghulam Ahmad, the Barelvi and Deobandi movements of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>, and the Nation of Islam, which arose in the 20th century. Differences between groups may not be well known to Muslims outside scholarly circles, and some groups regard others as deviant or not truly Muslim.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Islamic schools and branches” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
